1
00:00:02,967 --> 00:00:03,921
An equipment label.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:04,954
A repair note.

3
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:06,204
A changing status.

4
00:00:06,367 --> 00:00:09,938
Keeping these details connected is a practical challenge in a computer laboratory.

5
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:18,882
When records are separated, even a simple question takes longer to answer: which equipment is working, and which needs attention?

6
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:26,877
Meet LabTracker, our native PHP and MySQL application for equipment records and maintenance tracking.

7
00:00:30,433 --> 00:00:32,588
We begin with administrator sign in.

8
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:39,627
The password stays hidden, and the application opens a shared workspace for managing laboratory assets.

9
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:49,848
The dashboard summarizes equipment conditions and active maintenance, with this month’s completed maintenance costs above.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,347
These figures come from our sample records.

11
00:00:54,467 --> 00:00:58,501
Below, equipment condition and laboratory summaries add context.

12
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:04,387
The sidebar navigation keeps equipment, maintenance, reference data, and accounts within reach.

13
00:01:07,967 --> 00:01:10,399
Let’s follow one disposable sample.

14
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:17,436
We enter its asset tag, choose its category and laboratory, and add the brand, model, and serial number.

15
00:01:21,967 --> 00:01:25,660
After saving, LabTracker confirms that the equipment was added.

16
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:30,900
The detail page brings the stored information together in one readable record.

17
00:01:31,967 --> 00:01:34,463
Next, we search for the sample asset tag.

18
00:01:34,633 --> 00:01:40,137
The matching row gives us direct access to viewing, editing, and deleting that equipment.

19
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:47,343
Opening View lets us check the saved details before making a change.

20
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,888
We are following the same item throughout this demonstration.

21
00:01:51,967 --> 00:01:54,889
Suppose an inspection finds a flickering screen.

22
00:01:55,067 --> 00:02:01,104
We change the condition to Defective and add an inspection note, then save the updated record.

23
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:08,487
The detail page now shows the new condition and note.

24
00:02:08,667 --> 00:02:10,971
This verifies that the edit was stored.

25
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:15,348
Finally, we delete this disposable sample.

26
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:21,876
The application asks for confirmation, protects equipment with maintenance history, and returns to the equipment list.

27
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:28,591
For maintenance, we use our retained sample item.

28
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:34,475
A repair entry connects the reported issue and date directly to the selected equipment.

29
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:40,868
Saving an open repair marks this item Under Repair.

30
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:46,400
The equipment list and maintenance entry describe two parts of the same situation.

31
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:55,959
After the sample repair, we select Done, enter a completion date, choose Working after verification, and describe the resolution.

32
00:02:56,167 --> 00:02:59,398
The saved record preserves what was reported and completed.

33
00:03:02,933 --> 00:03:05,579
Administrators can also manage accounts.

34
00:03:05,767 --> 00:03:12,017
Here, a deliberately weak sample password is rejected with specific guidance about the requirements.

35
00:03:13,933 --> 00:03:18,029
We correct the password and create a disposable technician account.

36
00:03:18,233 --> 00:03:23,567
Passwords remain masked during entry, and the account list shows its role and status.

37
00:03:26,933 --> 00:03:30,325
We edit the sample account’s name, then deactivate it.

38
00:03:30,533 --> 00:03:35,675
Its status changes, preventing continued access while keeping the account record.

39
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:45,084
The source uses PHP password hashing and password verification.

40
00:03:45,267 --> 00:03:51,176
Protected pages require a session, and account activity is checked again on each request.

41
00:03:52,433 --> 00:03:54,813
Database operations use prepared statements.

42
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,105
Server-side validation checks submitted values, while CSRF tokens are checked on form requests.

43
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:02,415
These are specific implementation details.

44
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:06,932
The schema links equipment to laboratories and categories.

45
00:04:07,133 --> 00:04:14,109
Maintenance records reference equipment and the reporting user, connecting asset information with recorded work.

46
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:20,491
We also test the behavior: after logout, opening the equipment page redirects to sign in.

47
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:24,409
The earlier invalid password was rejected before account creation.

48
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:37,879
From creating and finding equipment to recording repairs and managing accounts, LabTracker brings these everyday laboratory tasks into a consistent workflow.

49
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:40,741
The people behind the project.

50
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:43,385
Jacinth Dhane Mendoza, main developer.

51
00:04:43,567 --> 00:04:47,227
Jerico Teologo, interface and user experience design.

52
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:50,969
Shena May Gadon, database design and data management.

53
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:53,085
LabTracker.

54
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:56,147
From equipment records to organized maintenance.
